cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use interface-region-imaging-spectrograph in your research, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
title: interface-region-imaging-spectrograph
abstract: >-
  interface-region-imaging-spectrograph, imported as iris, is a Python library
  for downloading and analyzing observations from the Interface Region Imaging
  Spectrograph (IRIS), a NASA Small Explorer satellite which has observed the
  Sun in the ultraviolet since 2013. It finds spectrograph observations
  through the Heliophysics Event Knowledge Base (HEK), downloads and caches
  them, and loads a sequence of rasters into a single named-arrays function
  array, which carries the time, wavelength, Doppler velocity, and sky
  position of every pixel along with physical units. The library also
  converts data numbers to photons using the effective area of the
  spectrograph, estimates and subtracts the stray light background of the
  far-ultraviolet spectrograph images, and helps plan observations by
  counting how many times a raster can repeat within a given window.
authors:
  - family-names: Smart
    given-names: Roy T.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9997-5515"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sun-data/interface-region-imaging-spectrograph"
url: "https://interface-region-imaging-spectrograph.readthedocs.io"
license: BSD-3-Clause
keywords:
  - Interface Region Imaging Spectrograph
  - IRIS
  - ultraviolet spectroscopy
  - solar physics
  - data access
